# # got roberta hallucination when i asked what is lecture 3 it gave data source regulator in the pdf this combined sentence is not even there , i meam it is there just above the slide different terms in an image not combined
# # . roberta retireved chunks nd combined them
# # The PDF page has two separate slides visible:

# Previous slide ending with Data Source and Regulator labels on a diagram
# Lecture 3 slide with Applied AI ethics: methodological aspect

# These are on adjacent pages — so when PyMuPDF extracts text, it's combining them into one chunk:
# ...Data Source    Regulator    Lecture 3    Applied AI ethics: methodological aspect...
# That's exactly why RoBERTa extracts "Data Source Regulator" — it's a contiguous span in the extracted text even though visually they're separate diagram labels from the previous slide.
# This is a chunking/extraction issue, not a retrieval issue. The chunk contains both the diagram labels AND the lecture title mixed together.
# The fix isn't in the QA pipeline — it's in extraction. We need to add page boundary markers during extraction so chunks don't bleed across page boundaries:
# python# In extract_page — add page separator
# return page_num, f"\n\n--- PAGE {page_num} ---\n\n{text}"
# Then in the splitter, \n\n--- PAGE becomes a natural separator that prevents cross-page chunk bleeding.


# Good question — and this is where most people misunderstand system design.

# 👉 Short answer:

# ❗ No — you will NOT miss keywords if you simplify the flow
# ❗ You will actually become more accurate and more stable

# 🧠 What you’re worried about

# You’re thinking:

# If I remove fallbacks → I lose coverage → miss answers

# 👉 That would be true IF retrieval was weak

# 🔥 But in YOUR system

# Your retrieval is already:

# FAISS (semantic)
# + exact match
# + keyword TF-IDF
# + reranker
# + protect_exact_matches

# 👉 This is already multi-signal retrieval

# ❗ The real issue right now

# You are doing this:

# Retrieve → Fail → Retrieve again → Rewrite → Retrieve again → ReAct → Retrieve again

# 👉 That’s NOT improving accuracy

# 👉 That is:

# ❌ adding noise
# ❌ increasing latency
# ❌ making behavior inconsistent
# 🧠 KEY INSIGHT

# ❗ Retrieval quality matters more than number of retrieval attempts

# 🔥 Why fewer steps is BETTER
# Current system (your version):
# Step 1: retrieve (good chunks)
# Step 2: rewrite → retrieve (slightly different chunks)
# Step 3: ReAct → may search again

# 👉 Result:

# ❌ chunks keep changing
# ❌ verification becomes unstable
# ❌ answers vary
# Clean system (recommended):
# Step 1: retrieve (strong hybrid)
# Step 2: rerank (clean top chunks)
# Step 3: answer (RoBERTa / ReAct)
# Step 4: verify

# 👉 Result:

# ✅ stable context
# ✅ consistent answers
# ✅ easier debugging
# 🧪 Let’s test your fear with examples
# Case 1: Keyword-based question
# "What is Lecture 25?"

# 👉 Your retrieval already uses:

# exact match → "25"
# keyword → "lecture"
# semantic → context similarity

# 👉 So:

# ✔ correct chunk is already retrieved in FIRST pass
# Case 2: Semantic question
# "What did Elon Musk say?"

# 👉 semantic retrieval handles it
# 👉 reranker improves it

# 👉 No need for rewrite loop

# Case 3: Hard case
# "Estimate of AI risk?"

# 👉 Even here:

# semantic → gets relevant chunk
# reranker → filters
# ReAct → extracts

# 👉 rewrite = optional fallback (not default loop)

# 🔥 Where rewrite SHOULD exist

# You asked earlier — here’s the correct placement:

# ONLY here:
# if retrieval_score < 30 AND precision < 25:
#     → rewrite ONCE

# 👉 NOT:

# ❌ every query
# ❌ inside ReAct loop
# ❌ multiple retries
# 🧠 Final architecture (your ideal system)
# 1. Hybrid Retrieval (strong)
# 2. Rerank (top 4–6 chunks)
# 3. RoBERTa (fast path)
# 4. ReAct (fallback if needed)
# 5. Verify (final authority)

# 👉 That’s it.







